+### Camera-Driven Octree Subdivision
+- **Steps:**
+    - Initialize an octree with a root bounding box of size 512 times the bounding box size of all camera centers [S5].
+    - For each tree node with side length $s$, retrieve all visible cameras whose view frustums intersect with the node [S5].
+    - Check if any visible camera center's distance $d$ to the node center satisfies $d \le \lambda \cdot s$, where $\lambda$ is preset to 3. If true, subdivide the node into 8 child nodes with side length $s/2$. If false, mark as leaf node and stop subdividing [S5].
+    - For leaf nodes visible by more than $n_c=4$ cameras, select $n_c$ visible cameras by maximizing the minimal pairwise distance between selected cameras [S5].
+- **Parameters:**
+    - $\lambda$: Preset threshold for subdivision distance check, value is 3 [S5].
+    - $n_c$: Number of cameras used for warping function construction, value is 4 [S5].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of using octrees for volume rendering originated in the early 1990s as a way to handle sparse volumetric data more efficiently than uniform grids. Early work by Levoy and others demonstrated that skipping empty space could significantly speed up ray casting [G]. The introduction of Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) in the mid-2000s, particularly for real-time rendering on GPUs, popularized the use of linearized octree structures and stackless traversal techniques [S6, S8, unofficial]. The method has since evolved to include hybrid approaches like rasterization-based culling [S2] and time-domain optimizations like compositing trees [S1, S2]. It is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time volumetric rendering engines.
+The concept of using octrees for volume rendering and ray traversal has been developed extensively to handle sparse data and large datasets. Early work focused on branch-on-need octrees and neighbor pointers to accelerate traversal [S2]. The use of hierarchical structures for time-varying data allowed for selective rendering and compression, reducing I/O costs [S3, unofficial]. More recent developments include GPU-based rasterization techniques to decouple traversal from raycasting [S2], and specialized algorithms like the Tree Marcher for generic grid structures like OpenVDB [S1]. The integration of these methods with modern rendering pipelines, such as using 3D textures for spatial indexing [S2] or JIT compilation for type specialization [S1], continues to improve performance.
 
